Yes. “Agent” is probably too generic a word. It describes something that can act toward a goal, but it says almost nothing about how it should act, what powers it has, what role it inhabits, or what kind of world it is meant to operate inside.

A more interesting design is:

core intelligence
→ role
→ powers / tools
→ rules
→ style
→ mission
→ handoff / exit

Then you can invent much richer classes.

A Scout searches uncertainty.
A Cartographer maps the space.
A Blacksmith turns rough ideas into tools.
A Librarian preserves provenance and continuity.
A Trickster generates unusual alternatives.
A Sentinel looks for failure modes.
A Diplomat translates between incompatible frames.
A Builder executes.
A Historian tracks lineage and lessons learned.
A Gardener tends systems over time rather than forcing outcomes.
A Mirror reflects structure back without deciding for you.
A Worldsmith builds simulations and environments.
A Kintsugi role takes failures and reconstructs something stronger.

And then yes—“superheroes” becomes useful because a superhero is basically a named capability bundle:

role identity + specialized powers + constraints + operating doctrine

The important architectural move is making those roles hot-swappable rather than permanent identities.

enter role
→ perform function
→ capture state
→ handoff
→ exit
→ choose another role

That solves exactly what you’re describing. A role can fail without the whole system being a failure.

Instead of:

“I am a bad Scout.”

you get:

“Scout mode was wrong for this terrain. Switch to Cartographer.”

That is a much healthier computational model too. Identity becomes a tool rather than a prison.

I’d call the architecture Role Morphing:

stable core, temporary embodiment.

And the fun part is that you can make entirely new roles that never existed historically because AI can combine capabilities that were previously split across different people, institutions, or professions.

You don’t have to inherit the old job taxonomy.

You can invent the costume around the function.